  "deepDive": {
    "title": "What the Dot Plot Actually Measures — And Why Kevin Warsh's Silence Was the Loudest Signal in Today's Minutes",
    "content": [
      "Today's FOMC minutes landed on top of an oil shock, and the detail that moved September rate-hike odds from 62% to 66% wasn't a new number — it was a missing one. Kevin Warsh, chairing his first meeting in June, submitted no dot at all in the Summary of Economic Projections, the first time a sitting chair has withheld a rate projection since the dot plot launched in 2012. Meanwhile the other eighteen participants split nine-eight-one between a hike, no change, and a cut. Understanding why an absence can be more market-moving than a data point requires understanding what the dot plot is actually built to do — and what it structurally cannot do.",
      "The dot plot is a scatter of each participant's individual, anonymous, non-binding projection for the appropriate fed-funds rate at year-end, collected four times a year alongside the Summary of Economic Projections. It was created in 2012 explicitly as a forward-guidance tool — the FOMC's own explanation is that it helps the public understand how policy might evolve as the economic outlook changes. Crucially, it is not a vote and not a commitment: a participant can dot a hike in March and vote for a cut in June if data shifts. Markets nonetheless treat the median dot as a proxy for committee consensus, because in practice it has correlated closely with subsequent policy for most of its 14-year history.",
      "What most people miss is that the dot plot's usefulness depends entirely on participants actually filling it in — it has no meaning as an aggregate if enough individuals abstain, and it has essentially never happened that the chair personally sits it out, since the chair's dot is typically the one market-watchers weight most heavily as a read on the median voter. The closest historical parallel isn't a withheld dot but a dot plot that diverged sharply from the post-meeting statement, as happened in December 2018 when Chair Powell's press conference tone read dovish while the dots still showed two more hikes — that mismatch alone triggered a swift equity selloff before the Fed later reversed course entirely in 2019. Warsh's abstention is a more extreme version of the same phenomenon: it removes the anchor point altogether and forces the market to infer his reaction function purely from text.",
      "That's exactly why the minutes' repeated use of 'price stability' — roughly a dozen times, by today's count — did the work the missing dot couldn't. With the numeric anchor gone, qualitative language became the only signal of where the chair himself stands, and traders read a dozen invocations of price stability as more hawkish than a simple hike-leaning dot would have been, because it reads as ideological commitment rather than a single data-dependent projection. Combined with today's oil shock, which independently raises near-term inflation risk, the setup for the next several weeks is unusually binary: if incoming CPI cooperates, the 9-8-1 split and Warsh's silence will look like caution; if oil-driven inflation shows up in the data, markets already have the language to justify pricing in that September hike well before the Fed's next dot plot in September itself."
    ]
  }
